Arrange the six boys in $6!$ ways. This creates seven gaps, including the two ends.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {\draw[black] (\x,-0.15)--(\x,0.15);}
\foreach \x in {0.5,1.5,2.5,3.5,4.5,5.5} {\path (\x,0) node {B};}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Place the three different girls into three different gaps in ${}^{7}P_{3}$ ways. At most one girl occupies each gap.

$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}6!\times{}^{7}P_{3}\\&=720\times7\times6\times5\\&=151200\end{aligned}$
